@page "/workout"

<PageTitle>Full workout</PageTitle>
<h1>Full workout</h1>
<p>The kitchen-sink page: one registration form that exercises the library's features side by side. The details section pairs an async availability check with a cross-field date rule and a coupon only the server can judge; the catering section is gated by pure UI state while its rule stays unconditional. Two ways out: <em>Save draft</em> runs the Draft profile, and <em>Submit registration</em> runs the Submit profile and then posts to the API.</p>
@if (IsHostedDemo)
{
    <p><small>This hosted demo has no server behind it — an in-browser handler answers with the same validators and the same response shapes the real API would send, coupon rejection included.</small></p>
}

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "Workout.razor", "Workout.razor.cs", "EventRegistration.cs" })">
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Contact email</strong>: required to submit.</li>
            <li><strong>Contact email</strong>: checked for a valid address. A committed change to the field discloses it, no submit needed.</li>
            <li><strong>Contact email</strong>: checked asynchronously as you type for availability (a fixed 300&nbsp;ms delay stands in for the server call). <code>taken@@example.com</code> is already registered. The rule is written with <code>MustAsyncMemoized</code>, so one address costs one check: for as long as the memo holds that answer, a pass started by any other field on the form reads it instead of queueing behind a second call.</li>
            <li><strong>Event name</strong>: required to submit.</li>
            <li><strong>Event date</strong>: required to submit.</li>
            <li><strong>Event date</strong>: must be a real date. A committed change to the field discloses it, no submit needed.</li>
            <li><strong>Early-bird deadline</strong>: must be a real date. A committed change to the field discloses it, no submit needed.</li>
            <li>The <strong>early-bird deadline</strong> must fall on or before the <strong>event date</strong> — a cross-field rule that runs only once both parse.</li>
            <li>Both date fields set <code>UpdateOn="InputUpdateMode.OnBlur"</code>: a native date input fires <code>change</code> per segment as you type, so the mode commits the value on every one of those but waits for the field to lose focus before notifying the engine, once the value has actually settled.</li>
            <li>Draft and Submit stay orthogonal, and the split decides what a <em>save</em> runs rather than when a message appears: malformed-value rules (formats, ranges, the availability check) sit in the always-on bucket, presence rules in the submit bucket. <em>Save draft</em> asks the validator for the Draft profile directly, so it answers about that bucket and no other; <em>Submit registration</em> runs the form's submit pipeline. <strong>Dietary notes</strong> is the one deliberate exception, a presence rule left in the always-on bucket, so a draft save answers about it too. What makes it a suppression demo rather than a completeness one is a different property: its rule carries no <code>When()</code>, so the checkbox hides the field without touching the rule (see below).</li>
            <li><strong>Required marks</strong>: the asterisks are derived, not declared. <code>FormidableRequiredIndicator</code> asks what the Submit profile demands, and the mark follows an unconditional presence rule — so Contact email, Event name, Event date, Dietary notes, Ticket tier and Venue region wear one, while Early-bird deadline, Description, Coupon code and Catering headcount carry the identical component and draw nothing: what rules they have are formats and ranges, never presence. Each attendee row's Name is marked from the same rule template that judges it, one mark per row; Email in the same row draws nothing, since no rule demands a value there. The glyph is <code>aria-hidden</code> and the input carries <code>aria-required</code> instead, so a screen reader hears the fact rather than a star.</li>
            <li>What decides when a message appears is engagement: the live channel evaluates whatever would block a submit, and writes its verdict only for the fields you have engaged — here, the ones you have committed a change to. A field nobody has touched stays silent however badly its rule is failing; engage it — type something, or clear something that was there — and it answers until the field leaves the rendered page (the session rows below hold their registration open with <code>KeepRegistered</code>, so scrolling one out of sight does not stop it answering), re-judged by every later edit anywhere on the form, with no submit needed.</li>
            <li><strong>Coupon code</strong>: no client rule at all — the server owns the code list, and its 400 lands on this field through <code>ApplyServerIssues</code>. Each response replaces the previous server verdict, so a corrected resubmission never leaves a stale coupon error behind.</li>
            <li>Before posting, the page calls <code>Normalize()</code>, which trims <strong>Contact email</strong>.</li>
            <li><code>Normalize()</code> also trims <strong>Description</strong>.</li>
            <li><code>Normalize()</code> collapses <strong>Event name</strong>'s internal whitespace to single spaces.</li>
            <li><code>Normalize()</code> trims and upper-cases <strong>Coupon code</strong>, so <code>welcome10</code> and the server's <code>WELCOME10</code> are the same code.</li>
            <li><strong>Catering headcount</strong>: a whole number between 1 and 500, checked only while catering is included.</li>
            <li><strong>Dietary notes</strong>: required, and deliberately unconditional. The checkbox gates the field's visibility, never the rule: hide the field and the rule still fails. A submit with no way to show that error blocks with a form-level explanation rather than with a message you cannot see — and once a submit has shown it, the summary keeps listing it, on screen or off, until the answer comes clean. Only a submit that goes through clears what earlier ones revealed, and that is what puts the explanation back in play.</li>
            <li>Editing after a submit schedules a background refresh that reconciles the whole form, while the pending indicator stays on the field you edited. Whether you see it at all depends on whether that pass has anything slow left to run: the availability check is the only rule here that takes any time, so a changed address paints an indicator and an edit anywhere else is answered too quickly to paint one.</li>
            <li><strong>Attendees</strong>: add and remove rows freely.</li>
            <li>Each row's <strong>Name</strong> is required to submit, and engagement is what discloses it: a fresh row stays silent even though the rule is already failing against it, but type into Name and blur, then clear it and blur again, and "Attendee name is required" appears with no submit needed anywhere. Nothing on the page or in the validator is wired for that — an ordinary submit-bucket rule on an engaged field is what the live channel answers.</li>
            <li>Each row's <strong>Email</strong>, when given, must be a valid address — a submit-bucket rule like the presence rules above, and disclosed the same way once you engage the field.</li>
            <li>The attendee list itself carries a collection-level rule from the always-on bucket: more than 10 attendees draws a warning ("More than 10 attendees needs approval — submission is not blocked"). Adding or removing a row is a committed change to the list, so it answers there and then rather than waiting for submit.</li>
            <li>The attendee list also carries a collection-level rule for having none at all: it draws an info ("You can add attendees now or after registering"), updating the same way. Both are advisories, unlike the error-severity rules above — they never block submission.</li>
            <li><strong>Sessions</strong>: 150 rows in a virtualized panel. Each row's <strong>Seats</strong> must be a whole number between 0 and 500 — an always-on-bucket rule, which is what makes <em>Save draft</em> enforce it too. Your edit engages the field it sits on, and that is what makes the row answer you rather than wait for a submit.</li>
            <li>Disclosure is render-registration, and virtualized rows come and go: the seats input sets <code>KeepRegistered</code>, which keeps a scrolled-past row's registration alive after it leaves the window. This page then goes further and sets a <code>DisclosureOverride</code> for the collection — an override answers before the registry is consulted, so every session row is disclosed, including rows Virtualize has never rendered. Validation always runs against the full in-memory model; only <em>visibility</em> is render-gated, so the override costs nothing.</li>
            <li>Clicking the summary entry of a row that is not in the DOM uses the summary's <code>FocusFallback</code>: the page scrolls the panel, lets Virtualize render the row, and the summary retries the focus once. A blocked submit's own auto-focus reaches the same row the same way, through the form's own <code>FocusFallback</code> — the identical callback, so no click is needed for it either. A field can also be missing because a control un-rendered it rather than because Virtualize has not reached it yet: <strong>Dietary notes</strong> and <strong>Catering headcount</strong> leave the page the moment catering is unticked, and there the same fallback answers the click on the checkbox that governs the field's presence instead of retrying an input that will not come back.</li>
            <li><strong>Ticket tier</strong>: a foreign control — a plain <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> this page renders itself. The renderless <code>FormidableField</code> hands it everything a wrapped input gets for nothing — element id, state class, aria attributes, and <code>NotifyChanged()</code> — so it styles, carries messages and takes focus from the summary like the rest of the form. Its presence rule sits in the submit bucket, and the form opens on <em>General admission</em>, so it stays quiet until you pick the blank option — which is a committed change, so the message follows that choice rather than waiting for a submit.</li>
            <li><strong>Venue region</strong>: the same seam from the other side — a native <code>InputText</code> with a native <code>ValidationMessage</code>, inside a Formidable form. Its presence rule sits in the submit bucket like the ones above, and the wiring is what carries it: the engine writes error-severity issues into the EditContext's message store, so the native <code>ValidationMessage</code> speaks the verdict ("Venue region is required") without a Formidable component in sight, live pass and submit alike; the engine installs a css class provider on that same EditContext, so native <code>InputBase</code> components pick up the same state classes; and a <code>FormidableFieldAnchor</code> registers a natively-rendered field so its issues disclose at submit instead of being suppressed as unrevealed.</li>
            <li>The one thing that shared EditContext cannot carry is click-to-focus: it addresses a field by the element id Formidable's own inputs render, and a native input renders none — so this page renders it, giving the input the field's id and the native <code>ValidationMessage</code> the matching messages id. The three aria attributes cross the same way, since a wrapped input takes them from a field context and a native one has none to ask. <code>aria-required</code> stands whatever the value does, because requiredness is a property of the rules. <code>aria-invalid</code> follows the field's errors, and the page reads it off the engine; a native <code>InputText</code> answers that attribute for itself from the field's messages, with the same <code>true</code> or nothing. <code>aria-describedby</code> names that messages id only while the <code>ValidationMessage</code> is rendering a message, since that component renders nothing at all when there is none, and an <code>aria-describedby</code> naming an absent element describes nothing — a wrapped input paired with a <code>FormidableFieldMessage</code> is never asked that question, because the list renders empty and stays put.</li>
            <li>One addition per concern is what makes the crossings the shared context cannot: <code>FormidableFieldAnchor</code> for disclosure, the id for focus, and <code>aria-describedby</code>, <code>aria-invalid</code> and <code>aria-required</code> for the screen reader.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            @if (IsHostedDemo)
            {
                <li>No server to start on this hosted demo — the in-browser handler answers as soon as you submit, coupon rejection included.</li>
            }
            else
            {
                <li>Start the API first: <code>dotnet run --project samples/Formidable.Sample.Api</code>.</li>
            }
            <li>Click <em>Submit registration</em> without filling anything in — the form opens with a ticket tier already chosen, catering already ticked and all 150 sessions seated at 0, so what is still empty is the boxes you type into, and every presence rule on one of them answers at once. The summary lists them, and every entry has somewhere to land: an entry that names an input focuses that input, native ones included (step 23), and the attendee advisory focuses the Attendees group, which owns no input of its own. A group scrolls into view and takes focus quietly under the mouse; reach the same entry from the keyboard and the landing shows an accent outline as well. There is one more kind of entry, the form-level one the gate raises, and it lands on the form itself rather than on any input — step 7 is where one appears.</li>
            <li>Type <code>taken@@example.com</code> into Contact email — every keystroke commits here, so each one hands the check an address it has never seen: "checking…" sits on that field for the 300&nbsp;ms the check takes, and then the taken message lands. Clear the box and type the same address again: the other messages come and go as before, but no "checking…" appears at all, because the memo already holds an answer for every address you typed through.</li>
            <li>Replace Contact email with a fresh address (the one from the last step is already taken), fill in Event name, Event date, Dietary notes and <strong>Venue region</strong>, and submit with the coupon <code>BOGUS</code>. The server can only speak once the client is clean, and the POST happens only after a valid client submit — so the coupon's verdict always arrives last: the server's 400 lands inline on Coupon code. Change it to <code>WELCOME10</code> and submit again: the new verdict replaces the old one and the registration is accepted.</li>
            <li>With everything else valid, clear <strong>Dietary notes</strong> and tab out — your edit engages the field, so the rule answers with no submit: "Dietary notes are required for catering" lands beside the box and in the summary above.</li>
            <li>Untick <em>Include catering</em> — the field leaves the page and takes its message with it, inline and summary alike. Engagement ends when the field leaves.</li>
            <li>Submit — the only rule that can block is the one on the field you just took off screen, and the submit that went through at step 4 cleared what earlier submits had revealed, so nothing on screen can account for it: the form blocks with "information that is not currently displayed is invalid" rather than with an entry pointing at nothing. That entry belongs to the form itself: the submit's own focus lands there, and so does a click on it.</li>
            <li>Leave the form blocked and type an event description into <strong>Description</strong>, then tab out — the box takes the confirmed border, which is the sign a pass has answered for it, and the form-level line is still in the summary afterwards. That line lasts for as long as nothing on screen explains the block, and Description carries no rule that could put an error there; a background refresh on its own never retires it.</li>
            <li>Re-tick <em>Include catering</em> — the note comes back, empty and quiet. Nothing has engaged it, and on the submit channel disclosure is a submit's doing, so the field renders carrying no message at all.</li>
            <li>Submit again — the note's own message appears, inline and in the summary, and the form-level line gives way to it. This is the submit the next two steps rest on: it is what puts the error on the record.</li>
            <li>Untick <em>Include catering</em> once more — the field goes and takes its inline message with it, but this time the summary keeps the entry. Compare step 6, where the same untick took the entry with it; what changed in between is that a submit has shown this error.</li>
            <li>Submit — the entry is still listed, with its field nowhere on the page. Once a submit has shown a field's error the entry stands until the answer comes clean, and the field stays watched until the form passes or resets. The rule never relented; only its visibility changed, and disclosure followed the submits. Click that entry now: the field it names is off the page, so the click cannot land there — focus moves to the <em>Include catering</em> checkbox instead, the control whose state is the reason the field is gone.</li>
            <li>Re-tick <em>Include catering</em> and fill a note back in before moving on.</li>
            <li>Put <code>nope@@</code> in Contact email and click <em>Save draft</em> — the draft answers about the always-on bucket, and the malformed address is what it names: the note you re-filled at the last step passes, and the presence rules are in the other bucket entirely. Now clear <strong>Event name</strong> and tab out: "Event name is required" lands as soon as that edit's live pass does, because you have engaged that field. But save the draft again and the status line still says nothing about it, since a draft save runs the Draft profile and that presence rule is not in it. Type Event name back in before moving on.</li>
            <li>Click <em>Add attendee</em> — a blank row appears with Name and Email fields, Name wearing the required mark from the rules alone (Email carries the same indicator component and draws nothing), and stays silent for now: a mark is not a message, and nothing has engaged the row yet, even though its rule is already failing. Type a name into it and tab out, then come back, clear it and tab out again — "Attendee name is required" appears as soon as that edit's live pass does, inline and in the summary, with no submit anywhere; clicking the summary entry focuses that row's Name field.</li>
            <li>Fill that row's Name, then click <em>Add attendee</em> ten more times, giving each a name (Email can stay blank) — once the count passes ten, the warning "More than 10 attendees needs approval — submission is not blocked" appears below the list.</li>
            <li>Contact email is still <code>nope@@</code> from step 14, and it is the only thing left broken — replace it with a real address, then check that catering is still ticked with a note in it and that <strong>Venue region</strong> still holds a value. Submit again: the warning does not stop it, and the status line confirms the registration was accepted.</li>
            <li>Click <em>Remove</em> on every row until none remain — the warning gives way to the info "You can add attendees now or after registering".</li>
            <li>Scroll the session panel to the bottom, set the last session's <strong>Seats</strong> to 900 and press Tab so the edit commits — the row objects as soon as that edit's live pass does — then scroll back to the top and click <em>Submit registration</em> — the submit blocks and the summary carries "Seats must be a whole number between 0 and 500" for a row that is nowhere on screen. The submit's own focus reaches it anyway: with no click at all, the panel scrolls itself, Virtualize renders the row, and focus lands in its Seats box. The <code>DisclosureOverride</code> is what lets the summary speak for that row in the first place: no registration is consulted, so it would report the issue even had you never scrolled that far.</li>
            <li>Scroll the session panel back to the top, then click that summary entry — the panel scrolls itself, Virtualize renders the row, and focus lands in its Seats box. That is the fallback: a click reaches only an element that is rendered and will take focus, so the page makes the row renderable and the summary tries again. Put the row back to <code>0</code> and that edit's live pass takes both the inline message and the summary entry away.</li>
            <li>Set <strong>Ticket tier</strong> to the blank <em>Choose…</em> option — choosing is a committed change, so the foreign select takes the same inline message and the same summary entry as any wrapped input on this page, with no submit asked for. Tab out and the red border joins them: a focused box wears the accent border whatever its verdict, so the severity one waits for the blur. Click that summary entry and focus moves into the select. The page renders the control; <code>FormidableField</code> supplies everything the wrapper would have — then pick a tier again so later steps start clean.</li>
            <li>Add a word to <strong>Venue region</strong>, which still holds what you typed at step 4, and tab out — the blur commits the value, and the live pass it triggers answers this field's submit rules along with everything else, so the native input takes on the same "confirmed" border a Formidable input shows: edited, nothing against it, and the engine able to vouch that a submit would not fail it. Nothing was wired per field to do that: the css class provider serves both kinds of input.</li>
            <li>Now clear <strong>Venue region</strong> and tab out — the native <code>ValidationMessage</code> shows "Venue region is required" without a submit: Formidable's verdict, reaching the EditContext's own message store from a live pass, rendered by a component that knows nothing about Formidable. The summary lists it on the same terms; click that entry and focus lands in the native input, exactly as it does for the wrapped fields above: the page hands that input the same id a Formidable input would have rendered, and the id is the whole of what the focus service looks for. Inspect the input while the error stands and it also carries <code>aria-invalid="true"</code>, the <code>aria-describedby</code> naming the message below it, and the <code>aria-required="true"</code> that was already on it while the field held a value — the page renders all three, because a native input has no field context to take them from. State classes, messages, disclosure, focus and aria all cross the seam here: the first two ride the EditContext both kinds of input share, and the rest the page renders itself — the anchor, the id, and the three aria attributes.</li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
</TeachingPanel>

<FormidableForm @ref="_form" Model="_registration" Options="_options" OnValidSubmit="SendToServer" OnInvalidSubmit="HandleBlocked" FocusFallback="RecoverMissedFocusAsync">
    <div class="summary-slot">
        <FormidableSummary FocusFallback="RecoverMissedFocusAsync" />
    </div>

    <fieldset>
        <legend>Registration details</legend>

        <FormidableField For="() => _registration.ContactEmail" Context="field">
            <div class="field">
                <label>Contact email <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _registration.ContactEmail" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_registration.ContactEmail" UpdateOn="InputUpdateMode.OnInput" /></label>
                <em role="status">@(field.State.IsValidating ? "checking…" : null)</em>
            </div>
            <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _registration.ContactEmail" />
        </FormidableField>

        <div class="field"><label>Event name <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _registration.EventName" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_registration.EventName" /></label>
            <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _registration.EventName" /></div>
        <div class="field"><label>Event date <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _registration.EventDate" /> <FormidableInputText type="date" @bind-Value="_registration.EventDate" UpdateOn="InputUpdateMode.OnBlur" /></label>
            <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _registration.EventDate" /></div>
        <div class="field"><label>Early-bird deadline <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _registration.EarlyBirdDeadline" /> <FormidableInputText type="date" @bind-Value="_registration.EarlyBirdDeadline" UpdateOn="InputUpdateMode.OnBlur" /></label>
            <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _registration.EarlyBirdDeadline" /></div>

        <div class="field"><label>Description <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _registration.Description" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_registration.Description" /></label>
            <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _registration.Description" /></div>
        <div class="field"><label>Coupon code <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _registration.CouponCode" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_registration.CouponCode" /></label>
            <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _registration.CouponCode" /></div>
    </fieldset>

    <fieldset>
        <legend>Catering</legend>

        <FormidableField For="() => _registration.IncludeCatering" Context="field">
            <label><input type="checkbox" id="@field.ElementId" @ref="_includeCateringInput"
                          checked="@_registration.IncludeCatering"
                          @onchange="args => ToggleCatering(args, field)" /> Include catering</label>
        </FormidableField>

        <p><small>Unticking hides the catering fields but not their rules: <strong>Dietary notes</strong> is required whether or not you can see it, so a hidden empty note blocks the submit with a form-level message rather than with one you cannot reach.</small></p>

        @if (_registration.IncludeCatering)
        {
            <div class="field"><label>Catering headcount <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _registration.CateringHeadcount" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_registration.CateringHeadcount" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _registration.CateringHeadcount" /></div>
            <div class="field"><label>Dietary notes <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _registration.DietaryNotes" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_registration.DietaryNotes" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _registration.DietaryNotes" /></div>
        }
    </fieldset>

    <FormidableField For="() => _registration.Attendees" Context="field">
        <fieldset id="@field.ElementId" tabindex="-1">
            <legend>Attendees</legend>

            <ul class="member-list">
                @foreach (var attendee in _registration.Attendees)
                {
                    <li class="field" @key="attendee">
                        <label>Name <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => attendee.Name" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="attendee.Name" /></label>
                        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => attendee.Name" />
                        <label>Email <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => attendee.Email" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="attendee.Email" /></label>
                        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => attendee.Email" />
                        <div class="actions">
                            <button type="button" @onclick="() => RemoveAttendee(attendee, field)">Remove</button>
                        </div>
                    </li>
                }
            </ul>

            <FormidableCollectionMessage For="() => _registration.Attendees" />

            <div class="actions">
                <button type="button" @onclick="() => AddAttendee(field)">Add attendee</button>
            </div>
        </fieldset>
    </FormidableField>

    <fieldset>
        <legend>Sessions</legend>

        <p><small>150 sessions in a virtualized panel: only the rows near the scroll position exist in the DOM at all, so their inputs come and go as you scroll. The wiring below keeps their issues on the books regardless.</small></p>

        <div class="scroll-panel">
            <Virtualize Items="_registration.Sessions" ItemSize="SessionRowHeight" Context="session">
                <div class="field" @key="session">
                    <label>@session.Title
                        <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="session.Seats" KeepRegistered="true" />
                    </label>
                    <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => session.Seats" />
                </div>
            </Virtualize>
        </div>
    </fieldset>

    <fieldset>
        <legend>Ticket and venue</legend>

        <FormidableField For="() => _registration.TicketTier" Context="field">
            <div class="field">
                <label for="@field.ElementId">Ticket tier (foreign select) <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _registration.TicketTier" /></label>
                <select @attributes="field.InputAttributes"
                        value="@_registration.TicketTier" @onchange="args => ChangeTicketTier(args, field)">
                    <option value="">Choose…</option>
                    <option>General admission</option>
                    <option>VIP</option>
                    <option>Speaker</option>
                </select>
            </div>
            <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _registration.TicketTier" />
        </FormidableField>

        <div class="field"><label>Venue region (native InputText) <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _registration.VenueRegion" /> <InputText @bind-Value="_registration.VenueRegion" id="@VenueRegionId" aria-invalid="@VenueRegionAriaInvalid" aria-describedby="@VenueRegionAriaDescribedBy" aria-required="@VenueRegionAriaRequired" /></label>
            <ValidationMessage For="() => _registration.VenueRegion" id="@VenueRegionMessagesId" />
            <FormidableFieldAnchor For="() => _registration.VenueRegion" /></div>
    </fieldset>

    <div class="actions">
        <button type="button" @onclick="SaveDraft">Save draft</button>
        <button type="submit" class="primary">Submit registration</button>
    </div>
</FormidableForm>

<p role="status">@_status</p>
